{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>At that time.</b> At the end of forty days that he mentioned, He became reconciled with him and said: ״פסל לך״ — hew for yourself." "<b>And you shall make yourself an ark of wood.</b> 'And I shall make the ark first, and afterward ״ואפסל שני לוחות אבנים״ — and I shall hew two tablets of stone,' so that I would have a place ready to put them when I descend with the tablets; for if I do not make the ark first, where would I put them? And our Rabbis of blessed memory received concerning this ark that it is the ark that Moses made, not the ark that Bezalel made in the work of the Tabernacle. This ark that Moses made was temporary, so that it would be ready to put the tablets into it immediately when he descended from the mountain, until Bezalel's ark was made — that one was made for all generations. This one of Moses used to go out with them to war; that one of Bezalel they would not take out at all — only in the days of Joshua did they take it out by divine word, and when they took it out on their own initiative in the days of Eli, against the Philistines, they were punished for it and it was captured; and because of it the Philistines trembled and said (I Samuel 4:8): ״מי יצילנו מיד האלהים האדירים האלה״ — 'Who will save us from the hand of these mighty Elohim,' ״כי לא היתה כזאת תמול שלשום״. In this ark of Moses the tablets and the fragments of the tablets were kept until Bezalel's ark was made, in which he placed only the second tablets; as it is written (Exodus 25:16): ״ונתת אל הארון את העדות אשר אתן אליך״. And what they said in Bava Batra, chapter one — 'there was nothing in the ark except the two tablets' and so on — is not an exclusion after an exclusion, but rather an inclusion: the fragments of the tablets were also kept in the ark. This was after the Temple was built, for in the Temple both the tablets and the fragments of the tablets were kept in the ark that Bezalel made." "<b>And I have already</b> written in the section of ויהי בשלח that this ark that Bezalel made was hidden away there in the Temple together with the flask of manna and the other things, and King Josiah (Yeshayahu) ordered them hidden beneath the tunnels of the Sanctuary. And the reason Josiah hid the ark more than all the other sacred vessels was for two reasons: first, for the exaltation of its holiness — for there is nothing among all the vessels as consecrated and exalted as it; and just as it precedes them all in the order of its making, so is it more consecrated than them all, for it is a likeness of the Throne of Glory and a dwelling for the Shekhinah, and the Shekhinah clung to the ark forever. Therefore in the Second Temple there was no ark, and when the ark was lacking, the Shekhinah was lacking. And since Josiah knew that the Temple was destined to be destroyed by Nebuchadnezzar, and the prophets of his generation were prophesying and crying out thus, and it was clear to him that all the vessels would be exiled to Babylon — therefore he had pity on the honor of the ark for its exaltation and wanted to hide it away. The second reason: he saw in the ark a sign of journeying — namely, that the staves which had lengthened in the days of Solomon became shortened in his days and returned to the same measure they had been in the days of Moses. And when Israel would enter the Sanctuary and see the curtain and the two staves of the ark, their heads were visible from outside the curtain like two breasts of a woman seen beneath her garments, and concerning these two staves Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Song of Songs 4:5): ״שני שדיך כשני עפרים״ and so on. And now when they became shortened in the days of Josiah and turned inward, Israel would enter and not see them protruding from the curtain as was customary — because they had returned to the measure they had in the days of Moses. And Josiah recognized from this that the ark was ready for journeying and was contracting inward. Furthermore, he saw that when Hilkiah the priest entered to perform service in the Sanctuary, he found there a Torah scroll that had come out from beside the ark of the covenant of HaShem into the Sanctuary, and he recognized from this that it was a sign for exile and for the destruction of the Temple. And this is what Targum Yonatan rendered (Ezekiel 1:1): ״ויהי בשלשים שנה״ — 'And it was in the thirtieth year,' translating: ״והוה בתלתין שנין בזמן דאשכח חלקיה כהנא רבא ספרא דאורייתא בבי מקדשא בעזרתא תחות אולמא בפלגות ליליא בתר מעלני סהרא בימי יאשיהו בן אמון.״ And since he saw these signs, he saw fit to hide it away. From this you learn that the essence of holiness belonged to this ark of Bezalel, not to the ark that Moses made temporarily. And concerning this ark of Bezalel our Rabbis of blessed memory said in tractate Eruvin, chapter הדר: 'As long as the ark and the Shekhinah are not in their place, Israel are forbidden in conjugal relations.' For if you were to say this about the ark that Moses made, which went out with them in their wars — how is it possible to say thus, for during the seven months that the ark stayed in the field of the Philistines, would Israel have been forbidden in conjugal relations and prevented from procreation? And likewise David, who said to Uriah (II Samuel 11:8): ״רד לביתך״ — 'Go down to your house.' Rather, it was concerning the ark of Bezalel that they said this, for it is the essence of holiness — unlike the ark of Moses, whose holiness was not as great."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:47:25.707345311Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}